IN vs EXISTS

INTERVIEW ANS ::==> IN is generally used when I want to compare a value against a set of values,
while EXISTS is used when I only need to check whether a matching row exists.
Performance depends on the database optimizer, data and indexes.

IN :

SELECT *
FROM Employee e
WHERE EXISTS (
    SELECT 1
    FROM Department d
    WHERE d.dept_id = e.dept_id
);


Agar Department table se IDs aaye:

10
20
30

to ye basically check karega:

Employee dept_id = 10? ✅
Employee dept_id = 20? ✅
Employee dept_id = 40? ❌


EXISTS :

SELECT *
FROM Employee e
WHERE EXISTS (
    SELECT 1
    FROM Department d
    WHERE d.dept_id = e.dept_id
);


Har employee ke liye check:

Employee dept_id = 10
        ↓
Department me 10 exist?
        ↓
YES → employee return



 | `IN`                                       | `EXISTS`                                  |
 | ------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------- |
 | Value/list membership check                | Matching row existence check              |
 | Subquery ke values se compare              | Correlated condition se existence check   |
 | Small result set me convenient             | Large/correlated datasets me often useful |
 | Duplicate values ka concept less important | First matching row milna enough           |

